<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    Adds one root certificate to those the application trusts, so that Android
    7.0 can reach the API at all.

    Found on 2026-09-19 on a Huawei MediaPad T3 running Android 7.0, which is
    exactly minSdkVersion 24: signing in answered only "A aparut o problema",
    and the tablet's own Chrome showed "Conexiunea nu este privata" for
    https://api.cyber-half.com/actuator/health. The API's certificate is from
    Let's Encrypt, and Android 7.0 does not carry ISRG Root X1, the root it ends
    in. That root reached Android in 7.1.1. So on 7.0 every request failed before
    a byte of it mattered, and the application could show nothing but the
    screens that need no server.

    The chain was read rather than assumed, through SSL Labs, since this
    laptop's antivirus re-signs every connection it makes: the server sends
    api.cyber-half.com, then YR1, then Root YR cross-signed by ISRG Root X1. One
    root is therefore enough. The file in res/raw was exported from the Windows
    trust store by its SHA-256 fingerprint, and checked with openssl against
    the value Let's Encrypt publishes:
    96:BC:EC:06:26:49:76:F3:74:60:77:9A:CF:28:C5:A7:CF:E8:A3:C0:AA:E1:1A:8F:FC:EE:05:C0:BD:DF:08:C6.
    It expires on 2035-06-04.

    The system's own anchors are kept, and listed first: this adds trust and
    removes none, so nothing any other Android version already trusts is
    affected. Cleartext stays refused, the default for this target SDK.

    TRIGGER for revisiting: the API failing on Android 7.0 again, which would
    mean Railway stopped sending the cross-signed Root YR and Root YR itself
    must be added here; the root approaching 2035; or minSdkVersion rising to 25
    or above, after which this file has nothing left to do.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config>
        <trust-anchors>
            <certificates src="system" />
            <certificates src="@raw/isrg_root_x1" />
        </trust-anchors>
    </base-config>
</network-security-config>
